# The law rank/composition_row_space.sqlc owes: within each fusion, the one way its figure moves
# and the ways that leave it still account for every part, and the part side and the fusion side
# agree on how many figures the parts can move. Two rows, one per half of that sentence.
#
# Per fusion, one plus the room
#   Within a fusion, the one way that moves the composed figure and the ways that leave it where it
#   is together account for every part: one, plus one fewer than the parts. The first subject holds
#   that on every fusion, against the parts rank/composition_kernel.sqlc counted, so a grouping
#   that changed its population on one side and not the other fails here rather than going quiet.
#
# How many figures move, from the part side and from the fusion side
#   The second subject is the one that crosses. `row_dim` added up counts the figures the parts can
#   move, one fusion at a time along the parts; the matching count in rank/composition_image.sqlc
#   counts the same thing along the fusions. They are reached through different groupings of
#   different relations, so their agreement is evidence, and a disagreement says one side is
#   gathering a population the other is not.
#
# The join to the grouping is outer, and an inner one would pass meaning nothing
#   Putting the part counts into an inner join's ON makes a disagreement drop the row, so the two
#   relations would part company and this law would see a smaller population rather than a fault.
#   `IS DISTINCT FROM` on an outer join turns a missing row and a wrong number into the same
#   finding.
#
# `row_dim = 1` is a claim, and it is held here
#   The column is a literal in the relation, because a fusion with parts always has one way to move
#   its figure, and nothing in a grouping can see that. This is where it is refused any other value,
#   which is what makes the literal a claim rather than a decoration.
#
# It needs a fusion with a sibling to test anything
#   One plus nought is one on every one-part fusion, whatever either count means, so a corpus of
#   one-part fusions would pass this law while saying nothing. The floor requires that some fusion
#   has room at all.
#
# Probe
#   examples/probes runs it as `composition_row_space` and `composition_row_space / the rank`. The
#   first reads `row_dim` as two in the relation, so no fusion adds up to its own part count and the
#   count of figures doubles, and both subjects fail together. The second leaves the part side
#   alone and adds one to the fusion side's count per composition, which only the crossing can see.
-- rank/composition_row_space.sqlc against rank/composition_kernel.sqlc, then against
-- rank/composition_image.sqlc.
SELECT a.slug AS law, p.subject, p.holds, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(algebra/roster.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT 'rank/composition_row_space' AS subject,
           count(*) > 0
           AND count(*) FILTER (WHERE x.kernel_dim > 0) > 0
           AND count(*) FILTER (WHERE x.row_dim <> 1) = 0
           AND count(*) FILTER (WHERE x.row_dim + x.kernel_dim <> x.parts) = 0
           AND count(*) FILTER (WHERE x.kernel_dim IS DISTINCT FROM x.kernel_by_fold) = 0
           AND count(*) FILTER (WHERE x.parts IS DISTINCT FROM x.parts_by_fold) = 0     AS holds,
           format('%s fusion(s), %s with room to move parts alone; %s whose figure moves '
                  'other than one way, %s not adding up to their parts, %s at odds with the fold%s',
                  count(*), count(*) FILTER (WHERE x.kernel_dim > 0),
                  count(*) FILTER (WHERE x.row_dim <> 1),
                  count(*) FILTER (WHERE x.row_dim + x.kernel_dim <> x.parts),
                  count(*) FILTER (WHERE x.kernel_dim IS DISTINCT FROM x.kernel_by_fold
                                         OR x.parts IS DISTINCT FROM x.parts_by_fold),
                  coalesce(': ' || string_agg(x.composition || '/' || x.composed_layer, ', '
                                              ORDER BY x.composition, x.composed_layer)
                                   FILTER (WHERE x.row_dim <> 1
                                                 OR x.row_dim + x.kernel_dim <> x.parts
                                                 OR x.kernel_dim IS DISTINCT FROM x.kernel_by_fold
                                                 OR x.parts IS DISTINCT FROM x.parts_by_fold), ''))
                                                                                     AS detail
    FROM (
        SELECT r.composition, r.composed_layer, r.parts, r.row_dim, r.kernel_dim,
               k.parts      AS parts_by_fold,
               k.kernel_dim AS kernel_by_fold
        FROM      (
            :compose(rank/composition_row_space.sqlc)
        ) r
        LEFT JOIN (
            :compose(rank/composition_kernel.sqlc)
        ) k ON k.composition = r.composition AND k.composed_layer = r.composed_layer
    ) x
    UNION ALL
    SELECT 'rank/composition_row_space / the count along the fusions',
           y.blocks > 0 AND z.matrix_rows > 0 AND y.blocks = z.matrix_rows
           AND y.parts = y.blocks + y.kernel                                         AS holds,
           format('%s figure(s) the parts can move, fusion by fusion, and %s fusion rows; '
                  '%s part(s) over %s fusion(s) leave room for %s move(s) of parts alone',
                  y.blocks, z.matrix_rows, y.parts, y.blocks, y.kernel)
    FROM       (
        SELECT coalesce(sum(s.row_dim),    0) AS blocks,
               coalesce(sum(s.parts),      0) AS parts,
               coalesce(sum(s.kernel_dim), 0) AS kernel
        FROM (
            :compose(rank/composition_row_space.sqlc)
        ) s
    ) y
    CROSS JOIN (
        SELECT coalesce(sum(i.rank), 0) AS matrix_rows
        FROM (
            :compose(rank/composition_image.sqlc)
        ) i
    ) z
) p ON true
WHERE a.slug = 'composition_row_space'
